{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 4af7d85745b20414e038130d04611790b0e2e76a, resting on all 48 submitted artifacts, notes/2026-10-01-laneB-git.md first. Both P1 findings in review db683a1d are addressed. Forty-two artifacts are byte-identical to the reviewed v4 head, including the lockfile; all six changed artifacts were examined, including every field of the new live-run evidence.\n\nP2 — Back off cleanup while a fork is busy (packages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts, cleanFork's state.kind === \"busy\" return, near line 534). forkState recognizes FORK_IN_PROGRESS and CREATE_IN_PROGRESS as busy, but cleanFork returns without advancing any open duty's next_at. Once due, nextDue remains in the past forever while that state persists. An independent real-SQLite diagnostic creates five ambiguous fork attempts, makes fork lookup return FORK_IN_PROGRESS, advances the clock, and calls reconcile ten times: every call leaves all five duties open, the identical overdue nextDue, and unchanged attempt counts. A caller scheduling its next alarm from nextDue therefore immediately retries again instead of using the documented capped backoff. The current Room alarm scheduler clamps overdue times to roughly now + 10 ms, illustrating the capacity risk; this is an integration consequence inferred from the caller, not a measured live alarm storm. During a slow or stuck creation, repeated metadata reads and alarms can consume the Room's capacity and multiply across lanes. Advance the unresolved duties on the busy path using the same capped scheduling discipline as absent/unknown outcomes. Keep every ambiguous step unresolved and continue later inventories; a busy snapshot is never proof of completion. Test both busy codes across repeated alarms and a restart, then allow the fork to become ready and verify late tokens are found and revoked without disturbing the current lease.\n\nArchitecture: each fork/mint attempt is now durably recorded separately, and an internal or transport error leaves the outcome unresolved. Unknown duties survive indefinitely; only explicit success or a refused-unchanged answer closes the remote step, and known token expiry can settle that token's debt. Independent tests confirm five late side effects are all revoked, a never-answered creation still stays watched after 25 hours and restart, and an applied-before-10400 mint is swept before ready while its duty stays open. There is no required functionality reduction or schedule-based waiver.\n\nSecurity: the two earlier false-completion rules no longer strand a late 24-hour creation token. Provenance, lease fencing, recorded-token preservation and secret redaction remain intact. The supplied live evidence records successful releases and rejected released tokens, but does not prove completion of an ambiguous request; the implementation correctly keeps such requests watched. The remaining busy-path defect concerns service capacity and dependable scheduled recovery.\n\nSimplification: removing applyBoundMs is the correct simplification because an elapsed estimate is not remote completion evidence. One ledger and one cleanup protocol remain easier to reason about than special-case retirement heuristics. Apply that same scheduling rule to the busy branch.\n\nValidation: clean isolated install of 196 packages and root strict typecheck passed; all 111 Node tests and 8 workerd tests passed. Four independent SQLite/fake-remote diagnostics passed: three confirm corrected unknown-outcome behavior and one pins the overdue busy-path defect. The new live-run file was read as submitted evidence only; no driver, live token creation, deployment or repository cleanup was run. Source in the builder checkout was unchanged. Lane A's full real-adapter and SQL integration remains a separate required review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7447f19b1c0ab72a743574ad450abbabc9f90da3","binding":"assigned","head":"4af7d85745b20414e03